n cleaned\n\ncleaned_headings = []\n\nfor item in extracted_headings:\n    text = item['text'] if isinstance(item, dict) else item \n    \n    cleaned_text = clean_and_validate_heading(text)\n    \n    if cleaned_text:\n        if isinstance(item, dict):\n            # Keep original metadata like page number, but update the text span\n            cleaned_item = item.copy()\n            cleaned_item['text'] = cleaned_text\n            cleaned_headings.append(cleaned_item)\n        else:\n            cleaned_headings.append(cleaned_text)\n\nprint(f\"Original headings: {len(extracted_headings)}\")\nprint(f\"Cleaned valid headings: {len(cleaned_headings)}\")\nfor heading in cleaned_headings:\n    print(heading)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.51228Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.512704Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.521547Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.51267Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.520681Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Original headings: 110\nCleaned valid headings: 26\n{'text': 'C H A P T E R', 'page': 1, 'size': 18.0}\n{'text': 'Digital Systems', 'page': 1, 'size': 34.0}\n{'text': 'and Information', 'page': 1, 'size': 34.0}\n{'text': 'INFORMATION REPRESENTATION', 'page': 2, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'The Digital Computer', 'page': 4, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'Beyond the Computer', 'page': 5, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'More on the Generic Computer', 'page': 8, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'ABSTRACTION LAYERS IN COMPUTER SYSTEMS DESIGN', 'page': 10, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'An Overview of the Digital Design Process', 'page': 12, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'NUMBER SYSTEMS', 'page': 13, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'Binary Numbers', 'page': 15, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'Octal and Hexadecimal Numbers', 'page': 16, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'Number Ranges', 'page': 18, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'ARITHMETIC OPERATIONS', 'page': 18, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'Multiplicand: 1011', 'page': 19, 'size': 19.9}\n{'text': 'Product: 110111', 'page': 19, 'size': 19.9}\n{'text': 'Hexadecimal Equivalent Decimal Calculation', 'page': 20, 'size': 19.9}\n{'text': 'F 5 Carry 9 15 Carry', 'page': 20, 'size': 19.9}\n{'text': 'E 4 6 14 4 6', 'page': 20, 'size': 19.9}\n{'text': 'Conversion from Decimal to Other Bases', 'page': 21, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'DECIMAL CODES', 'page': 23, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'ALPHANUMERIC CODES', 'page': 24, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'ASCII Character Code', 'page': 24, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'Parity Bit', 'page': 27, 'size': 11.0}\n{'text': 'GRAY CODES', 'page': 28, 'size': 14.0}\n{'text': 'SUMMARY', 'page': 30, 'size': 14.0}\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Our regex filter reduced the initial layout extraction from **110 items down to 26 items**, leaving us the core concepts of the chapter (e.g., `Digital systems`, `Binary Numebrs`, `Octal and Hexadecimal Numbers`)\n\nThere are still some artifacts related to broken multi-line like `Digital Systems` and `and Information` and Math traces such as `E 4 6 14 4 6`.\n\nWhile these headings serve as a phenomenal high-confidence data source, they present an extra NLP challenge: **sub-concept fragmentation**. For example:\n\n- `ABSTRACTION LAYERS IN COMPUTER SYSTEM DESIGN` needs to be decomposed to `ABSTRACTION` or `Abstraction Layers`.\n- `Octal and Hexadecimal Numbers` actually represents two distinct semantic concepts: `Octal` and `Hexadecimal`.\n\nThey are a great source and alternative we could dive more in the future to work with other strategies.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Relationships\n\nFor capturing relationships we will use two techinques: \n- **bootstrapping** using our concepts as seeds to automatially capture the most frequent relationship betweent them are\n- **Dependency Parsing** capture the relations based on what we see in the text\n\nThey could complement each other","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Bootstrapping\n\nThe general idea of bootstrapping is like a `GREP`, we match the strings that have our concepts, then we see what's between them, relationships will emerge as more common they are.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from collections import Counter\nimport re\n\ndef get_text_between(sentence, concept_a, concept_b):\n    pattern = rf'{re.escape(concept_a)}(.+?){re.escape(concept_b)}'\n    match = re.search(pattern, sentence.lower())\n    if match:\n        return match.group(1).strip()\n    return None\n\ndef bootstrap_relations(sentence, concept_map, top_k=20, min_count=2):\n    top_concepts = [c for c, _ in sorted(\n        concept_map.items(),\n        key=lambda x: x[1]['count'],\n        reverse=True\n    )[:top_k]]\n\n    between_patterns = Counter()\n    relation_examples = {}\n\n    for sentence in sentences:\n        text = sentence['text'].lower()\n\n        for i, concept_a in enumerate(top_concepts):\n            for concept_b in top_concepts[i+1:]:\n                if concept_a not in text or concept_b not in text:\n                    continue\n\n                between = get_text_between(text, concept_a, concept_b)\n                if not between:\n                    between = get_text_between(text, concept_b, concept_a)\n                    if between:\n                        concept_a, concept_b = concept_b, concept_a\n\n                if between and 2 < len(between.split()) < 4:\n                    between_patterns[between] += 1\n\n                    if between not in relation_examples:\n                        relation_examples[between] = []\n                    if len(relation_examples[between]) < 3:\n                        relation_examples[between].append(f'({concept_a}) [{between}] ({concept_b})')\n\n    print('Most frequent patterns between concepts:\\n')\n    for pattern, count in between_patterns.most_common(20):\n        if count >= min_count:\n            print(f\" '{pattern}' -> count: {count}\")\n            for ex in relation_examples[pattern]:\n                print(f\"    e.g. {ex}\")\n            print()\n            \n    return between_patterns, relation_examples","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.522546Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.522843Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.538664Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.522807Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.537745Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":19},{"cell_type":"code","source":"patterns, examples = bootstrap_relations(sentences, concepts_nc, top_k=20, min_count=2)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.53971Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.54001Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.582547Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.539975Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.581595Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Most frequent patterns between concepts:\n\n 's for a' -> count: 3\n    e.g. (voltage) [s for a] (digit)\n    e.g. (voltage) [s for a] (digital circuit)\n    e.g. (voltage) [s for a] (digital circuit)\n\n 'provides an analog' -> count: 2\n    e.g. (sensor) [provides an analog] (voltage)\n    e.g. (sensor) [provides an analog] (voltage)\n\n 'procedure for combinational' -> count: 2\n    e.g. (design) [procedure for combinational] (digit)\n    e.g. (design) [procedure for combinational] (digital circuit)\n\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":20},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"As we store the lemmas of our concepts we run into problems when we came across plurales of modified words of them, in this example \n> voltage -> voltages\n\nSo instead of capturing *for a* as a relationship we capture *s for a* as the lemma doesn't have that s, also in the other end we have repetition for the same stuff, digit and digital circuits it's a by product of matching the lemma of a single and compound concept","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def get_concept_pattern(concept):\n    base = re.escape(concept)\n    # Match base form + optional s, es, ed, ing\n    return rf'\\b{base}(?:s|es|ed|ing)?\\b'\n\ndef get_text_between(sentence, concept_a, concept_b):\n    pattern_a = get_concept_pattern(concept_a)\n    pattern_b = get_concept_pattern(concept_b)\n    \n    pattern = rf'({pattern_a})(.+?)({pattern_b})'\n    match = re.search(pattern, sentence.lower())\n    if match:\n        return match.group(2).strip()  # group 2 = between\n    return None","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.583748Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.584057Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.589272Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.584024Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.588544Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":21},{"cell_type":"code","source":"patterns, examples = bootstrap_relations(sentences, concepts_nc, top_k=20, min_count=2)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.590123Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.590661Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.647545Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.590624Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T23:18:47.646866Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Most frequent patterns between concepts:\n\n 'provides an analog' -> count: 2\n    e.g. (sensor) [provides an analog] (voltage)\n    e.g. (sensor) [provides an analog] (voltage)\n\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":22},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Even though we fix it we can't use really the core of bootstrapping as frequencies in one chapter of a notebook that we are using is limited, making patter frequency unreliable.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Dependecy Parsing","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test_sentence = sentences[5]['text']\nprint(test_senten